From: mklab <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH 45/45] TODO 17.3: /memory carries an `available`, and it is not a snapshot

Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

Patch 40 gave /memory a `reg`. Open Firmware also carries `available` -- the
UNALLOCATED subset -- which is what a client program consults before it claims,
and neither PC arch published one. Both now do.

IT IS REPUBLISHED ON EVERY CLAIM AND EVERY RELEASE, and that is the design
rather than a detail. `available` describes a CURSOR: a snapshot taken at boot
is a record that outlives its subject the first time a client allocates, which
is bug class #1 in this repo's own notes. libopenbios/ofmem_common.c does the
same thing for the arches that have ofmem (ofmem_update_memory_available); x86
and amd64 have none, so their bump allocators call it themselves.

    available  00800000 1f7e0000        (amd64, -m 512)
    claim 0x1000 -> 00801000 1f7df000
    release      -> 00800000 1f7e0000

IT IS DELIBERATELY NARROWER THAN "ALL UNALLOCATED RAM", and that direction is
chosen rather than accidental. Both PC arches allocate from ONE window, so any
RAM outside it -- everything above 4 GiB included -- is in `reg` and will be
REFUSED by claim. Advertising it would advertise memory the firmware will not
give you, and a client that believes `available` and then gets -1 has been lied
to. Under-reporting only costs a client an option it never had. Over-reporting
would be the lie.

An EMPTY window publishes a ZERO-LENGTH property rather than none: "nothing is
free" and "nobody has said" are different answers and a client cannot tell them
apart from an absent property.

The cell counts come from ONE reader, extracted here rather than copied:
memory_root_cells() now serves both publishers, so `available` is encoded with
the same counts as `reg` -- which patch 43 made per-arch, 2/2 on amd64 and 1/1
on x86. A window that does not fit those counts is left UNSET with a printk
naming it, never truncated: truncating an address names a different region.

All four assertions were watched to bite, and TWO OF THE FOUR CONTROLS were
themselves wrong on the first attempt -- which is this repo's own rule about
where the bugs are:
  * publish once at boot, never after -> "available is not tracking the
    allocator". (First attempt sed'd away the BOOT publish too, so the property
    was absent and an earlier assertion fired; the control never reached the one
    it meant.)
  * amd64 binds no cif-claim -> the missing-method failure. (First attempt
    DELETED the bind; the function went unused, -Werror stopped the build, and
    the track SKIPped having tested nothing.)
  * a refused claim republishes anyway -> "a REFUSED claim still moved".
  * hard-code 1/1 -> "not encoded with the counts that decode it".

NOT ASSERTED, said out loud: that `available` is a subset of `reg`. It is by
construction -- the window comes from the same sys_info -- but nothing parses
`reg` and checks containment, so that is a claim from reading, not a
measurement.

